Fix the panic-dump tail write (LBA 0 / EIO) + halt delay + halt summary.

0147's panic dump failed on hardware:
  ONC EMMC: root "userdata" ... 52919 MiB
  ONC EMMC: wr_fall lba=0 ...            (write at LBA 0)
  SWEET PANICDUMP: header write failed rc=-5
The header was never written, so TWRP kept decoding the previous boot's stale
ring.  The card init and PIO writes themselves are fine (0146's early BCB clear
works), so only the panic-dump base computation/validation is wrong.

0148:
  bsd/dev/onc_emmc.c
    - sweet_early_card_init(int force): the panic path forces a fresh
      reprogram of controller+card (takeover, CMD0/1/2/3/9/7/8) instead of
      trusting the state left by 0146/onc_emmc_init; a stale card/controller
      state is one plausible cause of the first write timing out.  The 0146
      BCB path keeps the non-forced call.
    - sweet_panic_log_dump() rewritten:
        * always re-runs the GPT scan, copies userdata first_lba/nblocks into
          locals and validates them: first >= 2, nblocks > 2*area,
          first+nblocks-1 <= sec_count (when known), first and base fit in
          32 bits (SDHCI CMD24 block address), and
          base = first + nblocks - SWEET_EMMC_LOG_LBAS (4100 KiB = 8200 x
          512-byte LBAs) is >= 2.  Any violation prints the exact numbers and
          returns without touching the card;
        * prints userdata first= / blocks= / base= / total= / sec_count=
          before writing, and stage+lba+rc on every failure;
        * writes the RING blocks first, the HEADER last, so a partial failure
          cannot leave a header pointing at stale ring data;
        * same 0132 layout/decoder compatibility, newest
          `sweetpanicdumpkb` KiB (default 32), `sweetpanicdump=0` disables;
        * failure-tolerant (prints and returns; reset continues).
    - sweet_pe_halt_restart():
        * `sweethaltms=<N>` (default 250 ms, clamped 0..10000) between the
          SWEET HALT line and the dump, printed for the camera;
        * after the dump, one compact summary line
          `SWEET HALT: summary cpu= target= entry= rc= mark= stage= init= sig=`
          from the 0147 globals (+ new originals), or "n/a" when the panic is
          not the SMP timeout.
  iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp
    - new C-linkage globals sweet_smp_last_cpu/init/sig; cpu = -1 until the
      5 s timeout sets them right before panicking, so the halt summary can
      always print the SMP state.

